<section xml:id="sec54-Rank" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Rank of a Matrix</title>

  <p>
    Recall that back in <xref ref="sec51-SubspaceSpanning"/>, we investigated two of the four fundamental subspaces as <alert>Null Spaces</alert> (<xref ref="def-NullSpace"/>) and <alert>Column Spaces</alert> (<xref ref="def-ColumnSpace"/>). 
    We also saw how we can find a basis for these two subspaces in <xref ref="sec52-IndependenceDimension"/>. 
    There is actually a cool observation about the relationship between their dimensions. 
  </p>

  <example>
    <p>
        Recall we did an example back in <xref ref="sec52-IndependenceDimension"/> to find a basis for the null space and the column space of the matrix <m>A = \begin{bmatrix} -3 \amp 6 \amp -1 \amp 1 \amp 7 \\ 1 \amp -2 \amp 2 \amp 3 \amp -1 \\ 2 \amp -4 \amp 5 \amp 8 \amp -4 \end{bmatrix}</m>. 
        <xref ref="ex-BasisNullColumnSpaces"/> will link you back to the work if you need a refresher. 
    </p>

    <p>
        If we try finding the dimension of the null space and the dimension of the column space, we may notice that they add up to the number of columns. 
        That is, <m>\dim \Nul A = 2</m>, <m>\dim \Col A = 3</m>, and <m>n = 5</m>, so
        <md>
            \dim \Nul A + \dim \Col A = n
        </md>
        Well this makes sense because a basis of <m>\Col A</m> consists of the columns that contains a pivot position, and a basis of <m>\Nul A</m> can be obtained using the columns without a pivot position. 
        So every column counts. 
    </p>
  </example>

  <p>
    This observation about the relationship between the dimensions is a <term>fundamental theorem of linear algebra</term>, as it links two of the fundamental subspaces together. 
  </p>

  <p>
    Terminology! 
    The <term>rank</term> of a matrix is the dimension of the column space, and the <term>nullity</term> of a matrix is the dimension of the null space. 
    Then we have the following theorem: 
  </p>

  <theorem xml:id="thm-RankNullityTheorem">
    <title>Rank-Nullity Theorem</title>
    <statement>
        <p>
            Let <m>A</m> be an <m>m \times n</m> matrix. 
            Then
            <md>
                \dim \Col A + \dim \Nul A = n
            </md>
            That is, the rank plus the nullity equals to the number of columns. 
        </p>
    </statement>
  </theorem>
  
  <p>
    The other two fundamental subspaces are the column space and the null space of the transpose. 
    That is, let <m>A</m> be an <m>m \times n</m> matrix. 
    Then its four fundamental subspaces are
    <ul marker="">
        <li>
            <p>
                <m>\Col A</m>, the column space of <m>A</m>
            </p>
        </li>
        <li>
            <p>
                <m>\Nul A</m>, the null space of <m>A</m>
            </p>
        </li>
        <li>
            <p>
                <m>\Col A^T</m>, the column space of <m>A^T</m>, aka the <term>row space</term>
            </p>
        </li>
        <li>
            <p>
                <m>\Nul A^T</m>, the null space of <m>A^T</m>, aka the <term>left-null space</term>
            </p>
        </li>
    </ul>
  </p>

  <p>
    If we apply the Rank-Nullity Theorem to <m>A^T</m>, then we can find the relationship between the dimension of the row space and the left-null space, as follows: 
    <md>
        \dim \Col A^T + \dim \Nul A^T = m
    </md>
    And if you think about the connection between the column space and the row space, they should have the <alert>same dimension</alert> since the number of pivot positions in a matrix should be fixed (unchanged through transpose). 
    That is, 
    <md>
        \dim \Col A^T = \dim \Col A
    </md>
    This is essentially part of the <alert>fundamental theorem of linear algebra </alert>, along with the orthogonal condition that we won't be discussing in this class but it is super obvious 
    (look up something called the "direct sum"), captured in the following diagram. 
  </p>

  <figure>
    <image source="sec54-FundamentalSubspaces-Diagram.png" width="75%"></image>
    <caption>Diagram of Fundamental Theorem of Linear Algebra</caption>
  </figure>

  <theorem xml:id="thm-FundamentalTheoremLinearAlgebra">
    <title>Fundamental Theorem of Linear Algebra</title>
    <statement>
        <p>
            Let <m>A</m> be an <m>m \times n</m> matrix with <m>\rank A = r</m>. 
            Then
            <ol>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        <m>\dim \Col A = \dim \Col A^T = r</m>
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        <m>\dim \Nul A = n - r </m>
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        <m>\dim \Nul A^T = m - r </m>
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ol>
        </p>
    </statement>
  </theorem>

  <p>
    There are other statements in the fundamental theorem of linear algebra captured in this diagram (i.e., <m>\R^n</m> is the direct sum of the row space and the null space). 
    Essentially, the fundamental theorem of linear algebra is a collection of results about many properties of the four fundamental subspaces. 
    In this class, all we care about here is the connection between the <alert>dimensions</alert> of the subspaces. 
  </p>
  

</section>